WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.680
Add interferon to human cells, and a set of genes switches on within minutes.

2
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:14.120
Interferon is a protein that warns cells of a virus.

3
00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:20.240
The genes still switch on when a drug stops the cells from making any new protein.

4
00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:26.680
So every part of the path, from the cell surface to those genes, must already be in

5
00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:27.900
the cell, waiting.

6
00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:30.360
What is the path made of?

7
00:00:32.083 --> 00:00:34.723
Interferon is a cytokine.

8
00:00:35.403 --> 00:00:40.483
A cytokine is a small secreted protein that tells other cells what to do.

9
00:00:41.303 --> 00:00:47.083
Each chain of a cytokine receptor crosses the membrane once, and it has no enzyme at

10
00:00:47.083 --> 00:00:52.763
all. Instead, its inside tail holds a separate tyrosine kinase.

11
00:00:53.523 --> 00:00:58.543
This kinase is called a JAK, and it is there before any cytokine arrives.

12
00:00:59.433 --> 00:01:01.473
Binding does one job.

13
00:01:02.093 --> 00:01:07.873
The cytokine sets two receptor chains, and so two JAKs, in position against each

14
00:01:07.873 --> 00:01:12.613
other. Now the two JAKs are close enough to phosphorylate each other.

15
00:01:13.193 --> 00:01:17.233
Each adds a phosphate to its partner, and both become fully active.

16
00:01:18.053 --> 00:01:21.473
A receptor with no enzyme of its own has borrowed one.

17
00:01:23.317 --> 00:01:28.897
The active JAKs now phosphorylate tyrosines along the receptor tails.

18
00:01:29.837 --> 00:01:32.997
These phosphorylated tyrosines are docking sites.

19
00:01:33.877 --> 00:01:37.337
A protein called a STAT waits in the cytoplasm.

20
00:01:38.157 --> 00:01:43.997
It carries an SH2 domain, a module that binds a phosphate on a tyrosine.

21
00:01:44.837 --> 00:01:49.617
With that domain, the STAT docks on the receptor tail, next to the JAK.

22
00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:55.770
The JAK then adds one phosphate to the docked STAT, on a single tyrosine.

23
00:01:56.610 --> 00:02:00.270
Now the same SH2 domain has something new to hold.

24
00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:06.270
It holds the phosphorylated tyrosine of a partner STAT instead, and the partner does

25
00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:07.270
the same in return.

26
00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:12.170
Two grips hold the pair, where one grip held the STAT to the receptor.

27
00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:14.770
So the same domain is used twice.

28
00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:23.370
The pair enters the nucleus, binds a specific DNA sequence, and switches on

29
00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:29.070
transcription. Now count the proteins between the receptor complex and the gene.

30
00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:34.450
There is one, with no second messenger and no chain of kinases.

31
00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:37.950
That is why genes can answer within minutes.

32
00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:45.750
So keep this rule: one protein, with one phosphate, carries the signal from receptor to gene.
